                <td class="header-logo-cell"><div class="header-logo-circle">SU</div></td>
                <td class="header-title-cell">
                    <h1>Faculty of Medicine — Suez University</h1>
                    <p>1st Year Medical Students | Musculoskeletal Module Physiology Exam</p>
                </td>
                <td class="header-info-cell"><strong>Time:</strong> 2 Hours<br><strong>Total Qs:</strong> 42 MCQs</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <div class="content">
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q1.</span><span>Excitability is defined as:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>The structural unit of the central nervous system.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>The ability of living tissue to respond to an adequate stimulus and produce an action potential.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>The automatic rhythmicity displayed by visceral hollow organs.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>The passive diffusion of ions down their chemical gradients during rest.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q2.</span><span>Which of the following tissues exhibit the highest excitability in the human body?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Nerve and muscle cells.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Bone and cartilage.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Epithelial and connective tissues.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Adipose and lymphatic tissues.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q3.</span><span>According to Erlanger-Gasser classification, which nerve fibers are thick myelinated, have a diameter of 2-20 µm, and a conduction velocity of 10-120 m/s?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Type B fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Type C fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Type Alpha-sympathetic fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Type A fibers.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q4.</span><span>Which type of nerve fibers is most susceptible and sensitive to structural blockage caused by prolonged deep pressure?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Type B fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Type C fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Type A fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Both Type B and Type C fibers.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q5.</span><span>In conditions involving acute oxygen deprivation (O2 lack / hypoxia), which class of nerve fibers displays the highest sensitivity and is blocked first?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Type A fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Type B fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Type C fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>All fibers are equally sensitive.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q6.</span><span>Local anesthetics preferentially and rapidly block which of the following nerve fibers due to their structural characteristics?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Type C fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Type A fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Type B fibers.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Myelinated somatic fibers.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q7.</span><span>What are the standard values for the Resting Membrane Potential (RMP) and the Firing Threshold of a typical large nerve fiber as illustrated in physiology charts?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>-90 mV and -70 mV.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>-60 mV and -45 mV.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>-70 mV and -55 mV.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>+35 mV and 0 mV.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-banner">
            <div class="key-banner">ANSWER KEY (PAGE 1) — Q1: B | Q2: A | Q3: D | Q4: C | Q5: B | Q6: A | Q7: C</div>
            <div class="footer-info"><span>Suez University | Faculty of Medicine</span><span>Page 1 of 6</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <table class="header-table">
            <tr>
                <td class="header-logo-cell"><div class="header-logo-circle">SU</div></td>
                <td class="header-title-cell">
                    <h1>Faculty of Medicine — Suez University</h1>
                    <p>1st Year Medical Students | Musculoskeletal Module Physiology Exam</p>
                </td>
                <td class="header-info-cell"><strong>Time:</strong> 2 Hours<br><strong>Total Qs:</strong> 42 MCQs</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <div class="content">
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q8.</span><span>As a motor nerve approaches a skeletal muscle fiber to form the neuromuscular junction:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>It gains an additional thick myelin sheath.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>It transforms into an unexcitable connective tissue.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>It pierces the nucleus of the muscle cell.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>It loses its myelin sheath and its neurilemma becomes continuous with the sarcolemma.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q9.</span><span>The primary functional role of the multiple mitochondria located inside the pre-synaptic terminal button is to:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Direct the passive leakage of potassium ions.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Supply the necessary metabolic energy (ATP) for the synthesis of acetylcholine.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Store calcium ions during resting states.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Synthesize acetylcholinesterase enzyme molecules.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q10.</span><span>Which specific channels located on the pre-synaptic axolemma open immediately in response to the arrival of an action potential?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Chemical-gated Na+ channels.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Passive K+ leak channels.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Voltage-gated Cl- channels.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q11.</span><span>Where is the acetylcholinesterase (AChE) enzyme structurally located within the neuromuscular junction?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Inside the pre-synaptic vesicles.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Bound to the intracellular troponin complex.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Within the synaptic cleft space.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Deep within the longitudinal sarcoplasmic reticulum.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q12.</span><span>The post-synaptic receptors located on the subneural clefts (synaptic gutter) of the sarcolemma belong to which category?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Muscarinic acetylcholine receptors.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (ACh-gated ion channels).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Alpha-adrenergic receptors.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Voltage-gated calcium channels.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q13.</span><span>Which of the following sets of properties correctly describes normal neuromuscular transmission?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Unidirectional conduction, a synaptic delay of ~0.5 ms, and susceptibility to fatigue.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Bidirectional conduction, zero delay, and absolute fatigue resistance.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Unidirectional conduction, a delay of 5.0 ms, and lack of chemical sensitivity.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Bidirectional conduction, a delay of 0.5 ms, and fast adaptation.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q14.</span><span>What happens immediately when the End Plate Potential (EPP) at the motor end plate successfully reaches the firing level?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>It causes immediate destruction of the muscle fiber membrane.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>It undergoes a localized decrement and fades away completely.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>It switches the post-synaptic receptors into adrenergic receptors.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>It spreads to both sides, opening voltage-gated channels and initiating a propagating muscle action potential.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-banner">
            <div class="key-banner">ANSWER KEY (PAGE 2) — Q8: D | Q9: B | Q10: A | Q11: C | Q12: B | Q13: A | Q14: D</div>
            <div class="footer-info"><span>Suez University | Faculty of Medicine</span><span>Page 2 of 6</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

