                </td>
                <td class="header-info-cell"><strong>Time:</strong> 2 Hours<br><strong>Total Qs:</strong> 42 MCQs</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <div class="content">
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q15.</span><span>Which pharmacological agent acts as a competitive (non-depolarizing) inhibitor by competing with acetylcholine for its nicotinic receptors?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Succinylcholine.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Neostigmine.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Curare (Tubocurarine).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Botulinum toxin.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q16.</span><span>What is the primary pharmacodynamic mechanism of action of Succinylcholine at the motor end plate?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>It binds to nicotinic receptors causing persistent depolarization, rendering the end plate unexcitable.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>It blocks the pre-synaptic voltage-gated calcium channels completely.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>It acts as an irreversible inhibitor of acetylcholinesterase.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>It actively accelerates the reuptake of acetylcholine back into vesicles.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q17.</span><span>A patient suffers from food poisoning due to ingestion of contaminated canned food containing Botulinum toxin. This toxin causes muscle weakness by:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Triggering a continuous, uncontrollable release of acetylcholine.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Destroying the nicotinic receptors on the post-synaptic membrane.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Irreversibly inhibiting the acetylcholinesterase enzyme.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Blocking the release of acetylcholine from the pre-synaptic terminal button.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q18.</span><span>Which substance is known to cause a dangerous, explosive release of acetylcholine from its pre-synaptic storage vesicles?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Curare.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Black widow spider venom.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Organophosphate compounds.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Botulinum toxin.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q19.</span><span>Pharmacological agents like Carbachol and Methacholine stimulate neuromuscular transmission because they:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Inhibit the synthesis of acetylcholine in the terminal button.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Block the pre-synaptic voltage-gated calcium influx.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Bind irreversibly to troponin molecules inside the myofibril.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Have an acetylcholine-like action and are not rapidly hydrolyzed by cholinesterase.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q20.</span><span>What is the critical clinical consequence of irreversible acetylcholinesterase inhibition caused by Organophosphates?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Continuous accumulation of acetylcholine leads to continuous muscle contraction and eventual death by respiratory failure due to inability of the diaphragm to relax.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Compete with acetylcholine for the post-synaptic nicotinic receptor sites.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Prevent the entry of calcium ions into the pre-synaptic terminal button.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Cause rapid degradation of actin and myosin filaments.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q21.</span><span>Myasthenia Gravis is a severe autoimmune disease where the human body abnormally produces antibodies directed against:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Voltage-gated sodium channels on the axon membrane.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Reversible anticholinesterase enzymes like physostigmine.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Its own motor end plate acetylcholine receptors.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Sarcoplasmic reticulum ryanodine calcium release channels.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-banner">
            <div class="key-banner">ANSWER KEY (PAGE 3) — Q15: C | Q16: A | Q17: D | Q18: B | Q19: D | Q20: A | Q21: C</div>
