        <div class="content">
            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q22.</span><span>Skeletal muscle is named based on its anatomical attachment. What percentage of total body weight does it constitute, and how many skeletal muscles exist in the human body?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>20% body weight and 200 muscles.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>40% body weight and 400 muscles.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>50% body weight and 600 muscles.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>10% body weight and 100 muscles.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q23.</span><span>Which of the following proteins represents the "thick" filament of the myofibril, containing heavy/light chains and cross-bridges with ATP-binding sites?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Myosin.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Actin.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Troponin.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Tropomyosin.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q24.</span><span>During the resting state of a skeletal muscle, what is the precise function of the regulatory protein Tropomyosin?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>It directly binds to calcium ions to initiate cross-bridge bending.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>It hydrolyzes ATP into ADP and inorganic phosphate.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>It wraps around the actin filament, covering the active myosin-binding sites.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>It anchors the thick filaments directly to the cellular Z-lines.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q25.</span><span>Which specific subunit of the troponin complex serves as the designated binding site for calcium ions, inducing a conformational shape change upon filling?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Troponin T.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Troponin I.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Troponin T-A complex.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Troponin C.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q26.</span><span>The functional and contractile unit of a skeletal muscle myofibril, defined as the distance between two successive dark Z-lines, is called the:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>T-tubule.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Sarcomere.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Terminal cisterna.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>H-zone.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q27.</span><span>Which element of the skeletal muscle tubular system is an invagination of the cell membrane, contains extracellular fluid, and possesses DHP receptors acting as voltage sensors?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Transverse tubules (T-tubules).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Longitudinal sarcoplasmic reticulum.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Terminal cisternae.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Dense bodies.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q28.</span><span>The terminal cisternae of the longitudinal sarcoplasmic reticulum play a critical role in skeletal muscle contraction because they:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Contain extracellular fluid rich in sodium ions.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Synthesize the neurotransmitter acetylcholine.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Store Ca2+ in very high concentrations and contain ryanodine receptors.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Act as the primary catalytic site for glycogen synthesis.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

