            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q29.</span><span>During the excitation-contraction coupling cycle, what directly follows the binding of Ca2+ to Troponin C?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Acetylcholine is immediately released from pre-synaptic vesicles.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>The troponin-tropomyosin complex moves, uncovering the active sites on actin.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Myosin undergoes immediate autophosphorylation by calmodulin.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>The muscle cell membrane activates its passive sodium leak channels.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q30.</span><span>Which step in the cross-bridge cycle is directly responsible for dragging the actin filament centrally toward the H-zone?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>The binding of a fresh ATP molecule to the myosin head.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>The passive detachment of the myosin head from the actin active site.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>The bending of the myosin head centrally (power stroke).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>The active pumping of calcium back into the longitudinal tubules.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q31.</span><span>What causes the detachment of the myosin head from the actin filament active site during the cross-bridge cycle?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>The absolute depletion of intracellular calcium ions.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>The binding of a new ATP molecule to the myosin head.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>The enzymatic destruction of actin by local proteases.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>The automatic phosphorylation of troponin I subunits.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q32.</span><span>Muscle relaxation is established as an active process because it strictly requires energy to:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Synthesize new nicotinic receptors on the sarcolemma.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Actively pump calcium ions back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the calcium pump.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Maintain a continuous flow of acetylcholine into the synaptic cleft.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Drive the passive sliding of myosin filaments away from the Z-lines.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q33.</span><span>Which condition is defined as a prolonged muscle contraction without relaxation, occurring in the absolute absence of repeated external stimulation, caused by sustained elevation of cytoplasmic Ca2+?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Treppe (Staircase phenomenon).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Rigor mortis.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Muscle contracture.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Complete tetanus.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q34.</span><span>Rigor mortis occurs after death because the complete depletion of cellular ATP directly prevents the:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Release of acetylcholine from pre-synaptic terminal buttons.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Functioning of calcium pumps, leaving active sites covered by tropomyosin.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Functioning of calcium pumps and detachment of cross-bridges, making the muscle stiff.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Passive influx of sodium into the dead muscle fibers.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q35.</span><span>What is the primary cause of muscular fatigue during prolonged, repeated contractile activity?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Accumulation of lactic acid which inhibits key metabolic enzymes, and depletion of energy stores like glycogen and ATP.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Excessive accumulation of acetylcholine inside the synaptic cleft space.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>An abnormal increase in the number of open ryanodine receptors.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Continuous hyperpolarization of the pre-synaptic motor neuron.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
