            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q36.</span><span>Which of the following properties is characteristic of Slow Oxidative muscle fibers (Type I / Red fibers)?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Low resistance to fatigue and very high ATPase activity.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Adaptation for rapid contractile activity and fine skilled movements.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Small fiber diameter, high myoglobin content, many mitochondria, and high resistance to fatigue.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Extensive sarcoplasmic reticulum designed for rapid release of calcium.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q37.</span><span>Fast pale muscle fibers (Type II b / White fibers) are highly adapted for which type of physiological activity?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Long-term posture maintenance (e.g., back muscles).</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Rapid contractile activity and fine, skilled movements (e.g., ocular and hand muscles).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Continuous aerobic respiration without fatigue accumulation.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Slow, sustained contractions supported by slow calcium pumps.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q38.</span><span>Why does "warming up" a muscle prior to exercise increase its overall strength and velocity of contraction?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>It increases the viscosity of muscle proteins and blocks enzymatic rates.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>It increases enzymatic reactions within muscle fibers and decreases the viscosity of muscle proteins.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>It completely depletes the calcium stores within the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>It transforms type II b fast pale fibers into slow oxidative type I fibers.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q39.</span><span>What is the physiological definition of a "Motor Unit"?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>One single myofibril and all the sarcomeres it contains along its length.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>One motor neuron and all the individual muscle fibers it supplies.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>The set of three regulatory proteins forming the thin filament complex.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>The network of T-tubules and terminal cisternae surrounding a single fiber.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q40.</span><span>The "Treppe" or staircase phenomenon is characterized by a progressive increase in the magnitude of separate twitches when a muscle is stimulated with a moderate frequency. What is its underlying cause?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Progressive accumulation of calcium ions in the sarcoplasm with each subsequent stimulus.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Progressive depletion of acetylcholine at the motor end plate.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Continuous irreversible inhibition of the acetylcholinesterase enzyme.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Rapid degradation of the post-synaptic nicotinic receptors.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q41.</span><span>When a muscle is stimulated at a high frequency such that it exhibits continuous, fused contraction without any visible relaxation phases, this state is termed:</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Clonus (Incomplete tetanus).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Treppe (Staircase phenomenon).</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Muscle contracture.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Complete tetanus.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="question-block">
                <div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">Q42.</span><span>In smooth muscle cells, what acts as the primary calcium sensor that activates the contractile mechanism, and what is the main source of this calcium?</span></div>
                <div class="options-grid">
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">A)</span>Troponin C, and the main source is the extensive sarcoplasmic reticulum.</div>
                    <div class="option correct-option"><span class="option-letter">B)</span>Calmodulin, and the main source is the extracellular fluid (ECF) via sarcolemmal channels.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">C)</span>Tropomyosin, and the main source is the dense bodies inside the cytoplasm.</div>
                    <div class="option"><span class="option-letter">D)</span>Troponin I, and the main source is the intracellular mitochondria network.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
